  <div class="page prose about-copy">
    <h1>Ryan D. Lewis</h1>

    <p>Ryan D. Lewis is a photographer, backpacker, and backcountry explorer whose work is rooted in Kentucky’s Red River Gorge and Clifty Wilderness.</p>

    <p>He didn’t begin exploring the Gorge in search of photographs. The photographs came from exploring it.</p>

    <p>Over years of hiking, backpacking, camping, and returning in every season, Ryan became increasingly interested in the places beyond the established trails—the unnamed tributaries, remote cliff lines, hidden arches, and blank spaces on the map. What began as hiking eventually became a game: find what was known, then go where others usually didn’t and see what was there.</p>

    <p>Sometimes, there was something there.</p>

    <p>That exploration led Ryan to discover and document remote natural features including <strong><a class="reference-link" href="https://kywaterfalls.com/cumberland-plateau/lilis-leap-falls" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lili’s Leap Falls</a></strong>, now identified on U.S. Geological Survey maps; <strong><a class="reference-link" href="https://kywaterfalls.com/cumberland-plateau/fletcher-ridge-falls" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fletcher Ridge Falls</a></strong>, a roughly 133-foot waterfall in Clifty Wilderness; <strong><a class="reference-link" href="https://www.redrivergorgearches.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Granddaddy’s Arch</a></strong>; and numerous other waterfalls, arches, and backcountry features.</p>

    <p>In 2025, after years of exploring the Gorge and surrounding country, Ryan completed a <strong>340.77-mile solo, self-supported thru-hike of the Sheltowee Trace</strong>. By the end of it, his relationship with the Gorge had begun to change. Exploring was becoming less about finding the next undocumented feature and more about sharing the places he had spent years learning—especially with his daughter, Lili.</p>

    <p>The photographs on Red River Gorge Hiker come from that life outdoors. A summer storm became a double rainbow over Eagle’s Point Buttress. Severe winter weather transformed Red-byrd Arch. A night in the backcountry ended with an unexpected sunrise. A line on a topographic map sometimes became a waterfall.</p>

    <p><strong>Red River Gorge Hiker is where those photographs and the stories behind them come together.</strong></p>

    <p>Some places are famous. Some are nearly forgotten. Some have changed since the photograph was taken. And a few weren’t on the map when Ryan first went looking for them.</p>
